# Mean–Variance Optimization (Baseline)

This notebook implements a traditional Mean–Variance optimizer using
historical mean returns and a shrinkage covariance matrix.

This serves as a baseline to compare against the Black–Litterman model.

import pandas as pd

from src.data_loader import load_price_data, compute_returns
from src.covariance import shrinkage_covariance
from src.optimizer import mean_variance_optimizer

tickers = ["RELIANCE.NS", "TCS.NS", "INFY.NS", "HDFCBANK.NS", "ICICIBANK.NS"]
start_date = "2019-01-01"
end_date = "2024-12-31"

prices = load_price_data(tickers, start_date, end_date)
returns = compute_returns(prices)

expected_returns = returns.mean() * 252
expected_returns

cov_matrix = shrinkage_covariance(returns)

weights_mv = mean_variance_optimizer(expected_returns, cov_matrix)
weights_mv

## Observation

Mean–Variance optimization is extremely sensitive to expected returns.
Small changes in historical averages lead to large shifts in portfolio weights.

This instability motivates the use of market-implied returns
and Bayesian blending in the Black–Litterman model.
